RECORD 2 — PRE-REGISTERED replication of the tail excess on the ambiguous band
written 2026-09-02 while the band is RUNNING and none of its results exist
=============================================================================

The development tail excess is suggestive and post-hoc (record 1). The ambiguous
band is a fresh sample of 353 items that has produced no scored result, so it can
turn a post-hoc observation into a pre-registered test -- but only if the test is
fixed before its numbers exist. That is what this is. At the time of writing 20 of
353 items are complete, none has been scored, and no forecast of either band has
been read for this purpose.

This is NOT the holdout. The holdout remains unspent under ADR 0031 and nothing
here touches it. The ambiguous band was always going to be scored; this record
only fixes WHAT IS COMPUTED ON IT and HOW IT IS READ.

STATISTICS -- exactly these three, on the ambiguous band ALONE, not pooled with
development, using the same z = Phi^-1(PIT) and the same 10-day blocks:

  1. tail ratio  RMS(z) / MAD-scale(z), with a cluster-robust 95% CI from the
                 moving-block bootstrap, 4000 draws, seed 20260813
  2. count of |z| > 2.5, against the Gaussian expectation
  3. count of |z| > 3.0, against the Gaussian expectation, WITH the number of
     distinct 10-day blocks those exceedances fall in

No fourth statistic. If something else looks interesting it is a new post-hoc
observation and is to be labelled as one.

PREDICTION, stated now:
  tail ratio      point estimate above 1.10, CI excluding 1.0
  |z| > 2.5       roughly 4-5x the Gaussian expectation
  |z| > 3         roughly 10-15x the Gaussian expectation, spread over at least
                  four distinct blocks
The development values were 1.2064, 5.0x and 14x. The prediction is deliberately
looser than those point estimates, because a replication that demands the same
number to two decimals is a test of luck.

INTERPRETATION, both outcomes fixed in advance:

  IF IT REPLICATES (tail ratio CI excludes 1.0 AND |z|>3 exceeds 4x expectation
  over at least four blocks):
    the tail excess is ESTABLISHED. Two independent samples, the second tested
    against a pre-registered prediction, and the multiplicity argument of record 1
    no longer applies because only one statistic was named. It also then bears on
    the Phase 3 form: a location-scale correction cannot fix it (record 3), and
    the Student-t successor is triggered on evidence rather than on preference.

  IF IT DOES NOT REPLICATE:
    the development result is recorded as a FALSE POSITIVE -- the 1-in-15 that a
    p of 0.066 predicts -- and the thin-tails reading is withdrawn, including from
    Findings #44 and from the reasoning in ADR 0032. The calibration ratio's
    disagreement with the PIT would then need another explanation, and "we do not
    know why 0.733 and a uniform PIT coexist" is the honest state to return to.
    Student-t is NOT adopted in that case.

  IF IT REPLICATES IN THE COUNTS BUT NOT THE RATIO (or the reverse):
    reported as partial, with the disagreeing pair named. This is a real
    possibility because the ratio needs a scale estimate and the counts do not,
    and it is written down so it does not become a choice between two numbers
    after both are known.

WHAT THIS STILL CANNOT SETTLE, whatever it shows: whether the excess is a defect
of the forecaster or a property of 5-day post-earnings returns. Both bands are
the same document type over the same event window. That comparison needs the
non-issuer corpus of Findings #45, not more of this one.
